Done — lease released, `impl/d1_pow2.c` and `strategies/d1_pow2.md` are the only files written.

**Technique ended on: PMU-guided memory-footprint surgery on the split-complex Stockham engine — compact (c,s)-pair twiddle tables at L≥1024 plus first-stage-pair fusion through an L1 tile at L≥4096 (3 array passes at 4096/16384). Best wallaby µs/transform B=1: 0.012 / 0.030 / 0.073 / 0.90 / 5.55 / 25.4 (L=32/64/128/1024/4096/16384); batched: 0.012 / 0.028 / 0.105 / 1.55 / 6.9 / 32.7. Rel L2 ≤ 3.5e-16 single-call; worst chain gate 6.2e-12 vs 1e-10.**

Context worth knowing: the Ice Lake reservation was alive for the first time (tryout.sh only *looked* dead — the login-node `squeue` shim checks the old gen campaign's heartbeat; the d1 heartbeat was fresh), so this round was measured on the scoring node itself. PMU there showed the large-L deficit was never port-5 shuffles: it was L1-fill bandwidth (60k line fills/transform at 16384) and dup-format twiddle tables (613 KB) blowing ICX's 1.25 MB L2. Fixing the tables took 16384 B=1 from 49.8→38.4 µs, and the pass fusions took it to ~33 vs FFTW patient's 32.6; on the node my worst r3 ratios moved from 1.64x/1.45x/1.51x (16384/4096/1024 B=1) to ~1.07x/~1.25x/~1.15x, with 16384 B=64 and 4096 B=256 now at or ahead of the best library. A complete Bailey four-step engine was also built, measured (correct, but 52.7 vs 38.4 µs — short tile loops doubled the load count), and removed, with the autopsy in the strategy record so nobody rebuilds it blind.
